e package"],[11861,"The way in for contributors: what Hoshimi is made of, what is authoritative, the conventions that apply everywhere, and what each development page covers."],[11425,"The Web UI (`hoshimi/webui/`) and the documentation site (`site/`) both use bun to install, build and test; CI uses bun 1.4.2. When `bun` is not on `PATH`, `dev.py` prints one line saying so and exits with status 127 rather than a traceback."],[8199,"The Web UI build files are missing and only the API is served. Packages installed from PyPI include the build; when running from source, build the Web UI first; see Development setup."],[10969,"The Web UI derives its root from the URL of its own script, so it needs no change under a prefix."],[11739,"The Web UI lives in `hoshimi/webui/`: a Vue 3 + Vite + TypeScript app following the Material Design 3 system it shares with its sister project Mizuoto. It talks to the server over HTTP only; the built `dist/` ships in the Python package and the server serves it as static files. This page covers its structure, its contract with the server, and the rules a change to the interface must keep, most of them enforced by tests under `src/`."],[11398,"The Web UI: bun"],[7930,"The Web UI's **Models** page and the Playground load the catalogue through `GET /api/v1/catalog/stream` (SSE): cached results come first, then each channel's fresh result as it arrives. At most 8 fetches run at once; a round waits at most 20 seconds, marks unfinished channels as timed out and keeps the models already received. The standard `/v1/models` stays plain JSON."],[7855,"The Web UI's **Settings** › **About** also shows the **Data directory**, **Config file** and **Database**."],[10408,"the Web UI's **Settings** › **About** shows the **Data directory**, **Config file** and **Database**;"],[10592,"The Web UI's **Settings** and **Channels** pages, or `PATCH /api/v1/settings`"],[9028,"The Web UI's Playground does everything through these routes, so a script can do the same: keep conversations, stream replies, run the tools and answer their approvals. Everything a model is sent (the system prompt, file text, image descriptions, earlier tool calls) is put together on the server; a client only says what to add and where."],[11904,"The Web UI's TypeScript types are generated from the server's OpenAPI document (`src/api/schema.d.ts`) and committed; CI regenerates them and fails on a difference. A change to a management API model means running `python scripts/dev.py typegen` and committing the result."],[13029,"The whole call"],[10857,"The whole configuration is validated before writing, so set keys that depend on each other in one go. To switch to proxy mode, for example, set the whole `network` object; `network.mode proxy` on its own is refused for lack of a proxy URL."],[12552,"The whole conversation scrolls as one. A tool's output, a compaction summary and a question being edited scroll on their own when they are long; once one reaches its top or its end, scrolling on in the same direction (the wheel, a trackpad or a swipe) moves the conversation instead of stopping there."],[8866,"The whole conversation; Hoshimi keeps no history"],[9437,"The whole of Hoshimi's HTTP interface: where the model, management and Playground APIs live and which key each uses, request checks and error shapes, streaming conventions, and the running server's /docs and /openapi.json."],[12073,"The whole patch is worked out against the files before anything is written: if one hunk does not apply, no file is changed."],[12206,"The workspace is the line the approval modes draw: reading or changing files outside it asks in **Ask first** and **Ask outside the workspace**, and goes to the review model in **Automatic review**. It is not a boundary for commands: a command can `cd` anywhere and touch anything the user running Hoshimi can."],[12205,"The workspace is where the tools start: relative paths resolve there, and `shell` and background commands run there unless the model names another folder. It is **Settings › Tools › How tools run › Workspace** (`playground.workspace`; `~` is expanded); empty uses `workspace` in the data folder (see Data folder and paths). It is created when a reply with tools first runs."],[11730,"Theme and design tokens"],[11322,"There are four workflows in `.github/workflows/`:"],[13467,"There are two ways to add a Kilo account:"],[12208,"There is no sandbox. Tools run as the user running Hoshimi, on that machine, with its files, credentials and network."],[15192,"These channels correspond to the free, trial or subscription allowances of AI coding tools and platforms. Most of these stations do not hand out an API key you can paste; they issue tokens through a browser sign-in. Hoshimi performs the sign-in in place of the official client, stores the credential as an account, and takes care of renewal and check-in."],[8845,"These endpoints serve OpenAI-compatible clients at `http://127.0.0.1:10600/v1`, authenticated with the model API key (see API overview). Usage examples are in OpenAI SDKs and curl."],[8201,"These errors come from Hoshimi's own access control; the request never reached upstream. Their body looks like `{\"code\": \"…\", \"message\": \"…\", \"detail\": {}}`. An OpenAI-shaped `{\"error\": {…}}` is an error from upstream or from routing instead; see the sections below."],[13442,"These IDs use the thinking menus declared by the reference implementation; other IDs follow the shared capability table (see Model behaviour)."],[15395,"These key-billed services also support `hoshimi login`; see their pages for details:"],[9666,"These options apply to this start only and are not written to `config.json`. An empty string for `--access-token`, `--model-api-key` or `--default-model` has no effect; the configured value is kept."],[12898,"These options are kept in the current browser and affect only it:"],[8485,"These provider options (in `providers.<id>.options`) adjust the behaviour:"],[11907,"These repo