 to 65535, then continues from 1024 upwards. The port actually used is:"],[9998,"This is the **Model API key**, not the tool access key and not an upstream Anthropic key."],[12099,"This lasts only for that terminal window. To keep it, and for a Hoshimi started without a terminal (a shortcut, a scheduled task, a service), set it for the system: on Windows, **Settings › Time & language › Language & region › Administrative language settings › Change system locale…**, tick **Beta: Use Unicode UTF-8 for worldwide language support** and restart; on Linux and macOS, put the `export` lines in your shell's profile, or `Environment=LANG=C.UTF-8` in a systemd unit. A program that picks its own encoding whatever the terminal says still prints in it."],[12532,"This page covers chatting. Letting a model read files, run commands and search the web is covered in Playground tools; the HTTP routes behind the page are in Playground API."],[7917,"This page covers the decisions the gateway makes behind each request: which channel gets a model name, what the model can do, what `reasoning_effort` finally becomes, and whether a failure means another account, a wait or a retry. Everyday use does not require any of this; when a result is not what you expected, the answer is here."],[9893,"This page covers the shared conventions; every command's arguments are in the Command reference, environment variables in Environment variables."],[14752,"This page covers two kinds of channel: local inference servers on your own machine, and the conventional API services built into Hoshimi that you reach with an API key (OpenAI, Anthropic, OpenRouter, DeepSeek, Groq and fifty-odd more). They share the same account fields and channel options."],[15193,"This page describes what Hoshimi's adapters implement. Each station decides its own free allowance, models and rules, and can change them at any time; the built-in model lists are only a fallback for when the station's listing cannot be reached. What actually